# {{ name }}

Experimental Rust Reboot app. The local SDK is a path dependency because
`reboot-rust-schema` is not yet published. Requires Rust edition 2024 and a
compatible canonical C++ `reboot/server/database` executable.

SECURITY: the current raw C++ Database listens on `0.0.0.0` without
authentication. Use only an isolated/trusted development network. The CLI
fails closed unless you pass `--rust-allow-insecure-database`; this opt-in is
not stored in `.rbtrc`. Only the Rust public gRPC host binds loopback.
Do not use this mode in production or expose the sidecar to an untrusted network.

```sh
export RBT_RUST_DATABASE_BINARY=/absolute/path/to/reboot/server/database
rbt dev run --rust-allow-insecure-database
# Another terminal, from this project:
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- create
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- greet
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- read
```

Cargo `build.rs` compiles `api/{{ name }}/v1/hello_world.proto` and emits both
Tonic bindings and Reboot Database adapters/external typed clients in `OUT_DIR`.
The app mounts the generated service through `ApplicationHost`; constructor,
writer and reader use `DatabaseActorStore`. Re-running Create on an existing
actor is an error; use a stable idempotency key to replay one logical write:
`client greet hello 11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111`.

`rbt dev run --rust-allow-insecure-database` owns one Database process and one Rust host. Source/proto
changes rebuild/restart the host while retaining the sidecar; restarting the
command reopens `.rbt/dev/{{ name }}/rust/rocksdb`. Ctrl-C reaps owned children.
Set `RBT_RUST_URL` on the client when overriding `--port`. Logs are in the same
Rust state directory. `--terminate-after-health-check` performs an actual gRPC
health RPC then stops both processes. Run `rbt dev run --rust-allow-insecure-database --terminate-after-health-check` for a CLI startup smoke test.

This is direct local gRPC, not Envoy/HTTP/React, cloud deployment, multi-server
placement, cross-actor transactions, workflows or reactive-reader parity.
Distributed placement is deliberately absent: all local actors use one shard.
Python dev runtime switches are rejected, not silently ignored. Rust state is
compatible with `rbt dev expunge --application-name={{ name }}`.

## Independent reactive readers for multiple actors

The ordinary greeting service can host an explicitly configured allowlist of
reactive actors. Use the generated typed client to obtain canonical references:

```sh
ALPHA=$(cargo run --quiet --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- state-ref alpha)
BETA=$(cargo run --quiet --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- state-ref beta)
export RBT_RUST_REACTIVE_ACTORS="$ALPHA,$BETA"
rbt dev run --rust-allow-insecure-database
# Another shell: use the same references and RBT_RUST_URL when changing the port.
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- create "$ALPHA"
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- create "$BETA"
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- watch "$ALPHA" 2
# In another shell, greet ALPHA to deliver its next changed snapshot.
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- greet "$ALPHA"
```

Each subscription receives an initial snapshot and changed values for its exact
actor. Writes to BETA do not invalidate ALPHA. Owners start before readiness;
a missing actor fails rather than constructing state. Unknown subscription
references are denied even if ordinary RPCs may construct them. The registry
admits at most 64 actors and 64 simultaneous subscriptions across the host;
duplicate references, malformed configuration and an empty allowlist fail closed.
The allowlist is read at startup; changing it requires restarting `rbt dev run`.

The default scaffold without this environment variable is unchanged. This is
local routing for independent unary readers, not cross-actor dependency tracking,
atomic multi-actor snapshots, distributed watches or durable stream resume.
Dropping a stream releases admission; restart requires an explicit fresh watch.

### Opt-in selected-source view

Set `RBT_RUST_REACTIVE_COMPOSITION=1` alongside the actor allowlist to enable
bounded one-hop composition. Register source actors plus a selector, construct
them through the public client, then select a source:

```sh
SELECTOR=$(cargo run --quiet --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- state-ref selector)
export RBT_RUST_REACTIVE_ACTORS="$ALPHA,$BETA,$SELECTOR"
export RBT_RUST_REACTIVE_COMPOSITION=1
rbt dev run --rust-allow-insecure-database
# In another shell after startup:
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- create "$SELECTOR"
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- select-source "$SELECTOR" "$ALPHA"
cargo run --manifest-path backend/Cargo.toml --bin client -- watch "$SELECTOR" 2
# Greet ALPHA in another shell to update the selector's view.
```

The selector persists its source. `select-source "$SELECTOR" -` clears it.
A selected-source view requires a composed reactive scope; its ordinary unary
reader fails rather than silently returning the selector's local count. Selecting
a source that is itself a view rejects nested composition. Unknown/unregistered,
self and mismatched-type dependencies fail closed. Reconnect starts a fresh view
from persisted state; it is not event replay.

SDK handlers can override the generated `{reader}_with_reader_context` hook and
call generated `{reader}_read_local` helpers. Each evaluation retains at most
eight distinct direct dependencies, registers revisions before reading, and
retires unused actors even when its output is unchanged. Target authorization is
rerun with original credentials. Snapshot leases are released before composed
callbacks, preventing cross-actor lease cycles. Read errors or canceled reads
poison the evaluation even if application code catches them; closing or dropping
an evaluation cancels escaped in-flight reads.

This is a same-host, same-Database, non-atomic view—not a recursive DAG,
distributed invalidation, transparent retry or durable stream-resume contract.

### Ordinary composed reader RPCs

With composition enabled, the ordinary generated `NumGreetings` unary RPC and
`client read <selector-ref>` evaluate the same authorized one-hop view as watch.
Each request takes one fresh snapshot and drops its cursor before returning;
there is no retained subscription or background dependency producer.

A configured adapter requires exact registered roots. Unknown/type-mismatched
roots, routing/auth/dependency failures and expired deadlines reject; they never
retry through the ordinary local getter. Without registry attachment, legacy
ordinary reader behavior is unchanged. Include every desired unary root in the
explicit allowlist. The scaffold explicitly permits its existing raw `hello`
actor as an authorized legacy unary root; it does not reinterpret `hello` as
a canonical actor reference or automatically create/register an actor.

For custom apps, finalize authorization on the baseline adapter, register its
`local_readers` clones, enable registry composition, then attach a completed
registry clone with `adapter.with_reader_registry(registry.clone())?` before
serving; install the original registry on `ApplicationHost` as well. Attachment
checks the exact Database endpoint and opaque generated handler/policy version.
For deliberately retained non-composed roots, an explicit bounded exact list
can be configured using `with_legacy_unary_roots`; registered roots cannot be
shadowed by that list. Default custom adapter attachment is strict.
Changing adapter authorization detaches an old registry and rotates its identity;
re-register/re-attach the updated adapter rather than serving stale policies.
Transaction/workflow root composition remains unsupported.

Unary evaluations share the host-wide 64-slot stream/evaluation admission pool.
The inbound gRPC timeout is parsed once and enforced as an absolute deadline over
admission, authenticated snapshot Load, dependencies and result decoding. Drop or
deadline cancellation releases permits and seals escaped reader contexts. Root
extensions are retained; only existing trusted provenance and explicit target
scope are delegated to leaf readers. A terminal evaluated `Unavailable` retains
its status code and an SDK marker so generated reader clients do not reinterpret
it as a disconnected transport and automatically start a new evaluation. Mutation/transaction metadata cannot grant
composed reader authority. Separate actor Loads are not an atomic multi-actor
snapshot. Remote invalidation, nested DAGs and durable resume remain unavailable.
